WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.040
How can a cell count?

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.820
Calcium often rises in brief spikes.

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.760
In many cell types, a stronger stimulus gives more frequent spikes of about the same

4
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.780
height. To read that, a cell needs a part that switches on fast and off slowly.

5
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:27.813
Picture a bucket with a small hole, topped up by one cup of water at each spike.

6
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.493
Fill it rarely, and it drains between cups.

7
00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:34.353
Fill it often, and the level climbs.

8
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:39.857
Take a decoder that loses its activity with a fixed half-time.

9
00:00:40.477 --> 00:00:46.297
The fraction left at the next spike is one half, raised to the gap between spikes

10
00:00:46.557 --> 00:00:47.877
divided by the half-time.

11
00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:53.937
With a half-time of 20 seconds, spikes a minute apart leave one eighth of the

12
00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:59.017
activity. Spikes 10 seconds apart leave about 70 percent to build on.

13
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:06.977
In 1998, Ricardo Dolmetsch, Keli Xu and Richard Lewis tested human T cells.

14
00:01:07.957 --> 00:01:12.377
They imposed calcium spikes of chosen spacing on a line of these cells.

15
00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:20.317
Closely spaced spikes activated three transcription factors, NFAT and NF-κB among

16
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:24.997
them. Widely spaced spikes activated only NF-κB.

17
00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:29.177
A likely reason is how fast each one switches off.

18
00:01:30.057 --> 00:01:33.837
NFAT leaves the nucleus within minutes once calcium falls.

19
00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:38.557
In B cells, NF-κB was found to stay in the nucleus.

20
00:01:39.097 --> 00:01:42.757
It stayed for more than 16 minutes after a brief calcium spike.

21
00:01:43.637 --> 00:01:47.037
So a decoder that holds on between spikes can count.
